= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $H_A=\mathcal C(-,A)$ for a <representable presheaf>. The contravariant <Yoneda lemma> states that, for every <presheaf on a category> $X$, evaluation at the identity is a bijection
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Nat}(H_A,X)\longrightarrow X(A),\qquad\alpha\longmapsto\alpha_A(1_A),}
$$
natural in both $A$ and $X$. Smallness of $\mathcal C$ ensures the relevant families and natural-transformation collections are sets.

For $x\in X(A)$ define $\alpha^x_B(h)=X(h)(x)$ for each $h:B\to A$. This family is a <natural transformation>: for $u:B'\to B$, $X(u)\alpha^x_B(h)=X(u)X(h)x=X(hu)x=\alpha^x_{B'}(hu)$. Its identity value is $x$. Conversely, naturality of any $\alpha$ at $h:B\to A$ gives $\alpha_B(h)=X(h)\alpha_A(1_A)$. Thus evaluation and the displayed construction are inverse.

For a map $v:A\to A'$, precomposing a transformation $H_{A'}\to X$ with postcomposition $H_A\to H_{A'}$ corresponds to applying $X(v):X(A')\to X(A)$. For a transformation $\tau:X\to Y$, postcomposition corresponds to $x\mapsto\tau_A(x)$. These identities prove the stated <naturality> in both variables.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Yoneda embedding> is the <functor> $H_\bullet:\mathcal C\to[\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}},\mathbf{Set}]$ sending $A$ to $H_A=\mathcal C(-,A)$ and $f:A\to B$ to postcomposition, $(H_f)_U(h)=fh$. Composition and identities are preserved by associativity and the identity laws in $\mathcal C$.

Apply the <Yoneda lemma> with $X=H_B$. It gives a bijection $\operatorname{Nat}(H_A,H_B)\cong\mathcal C(A,B)$. The inverse sends $f$ precisely to $H_f$, since $H_B(h)(f)=fh$. Hence every transformation between the representables comes from exactly one morphism of $\mathcal C$. This proves that **$H_\bullet$ is a full and faithful <functor>**.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If $f:A\to B$ is a <monomorphism>, every component $\mathcal C(U,A)\to\mathcal C(U,B)$ of $H_f$ is injective by left cancellation. Thus if $H_f\alpha=H_f\beta$ for two <natural transformations> into $H_A$, componentwise injectivity forces $\alpha=\beta$, so $H_f$ is monic.

Conversely suppose $H_f$ is monic, and let $a,b:U\to A$ satisfy $fa=fb$. Functoriality gives $H_fH_a=H_fH_b$, hence $H_a=H_b$. The <Yoneda embedding> is faithful, so $a=b$. This proves
$$
\boxed{H_f\text{ is monic if and only if }f\text{ is monic}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

If $f:A\to B$ has a section $g:B\to A$, functoriality gives $H_fH_g=1_{H_B}$. Thus $H_f$ is a <split epimorphism> and in particular an <epimorphism>.

For the converse, justify the needed pointwise surjectivity directly. Let $I\subseteq H_B$ be the image subpresheaf of $H_f$. Form a new presheaf by taking, at every object, two copies of $H_B(U)$ and identifying their elements in $I(U)$. Restriction maps descend because $I$ is a subpresheaf. The two canonical transformations $j_1,j_2:H_B\to H_B\amalg_IH_B$ agree after $H_f$. If any component of $I$ is proper, an element outside it distinguishes $j_1$ from $j_2$. Therefore epimorphicity forces every component of $H_f$ to be surjective.

In particular, its component at $B$ has $1_B$ in its image: there is $g:B\to A$ with $fg=1_B$. This proves the <Yoneda embedding detects split epimorphisms> criterion
$$
\boxed{H_f\text{ is epic if and only if }f\text{ is split epic}.}
$$
Ordinary epimorphicity of $f$ alone would not suffice.

